    "OtherAbstract": {
      "Type": "PIP",
      "Language": "eng",
      "AbstractText": "The tolerability and effectiveness of depot medroxyprogesterone acetate (DMPA), compared with an oral contraceptive (OC) combined with low-dose danazol, in the long-term treatment of pelvic pain in women with endometriosis were evaluated in a randomized clinical trial. 40 women were allocated to each treatment regimen. At the 1-year assessment, a significant decrease was observed in all symptom scores of the visual analog and the verbal rating scale in both study groups. Only pain at menstruation was significantly greater in women in the OC group (because of the absence of regular flow in subjects in the DMPA group). In the DMPA group, 1 woman (2.5%) was very satisfied with her treatment, 28 (70%) were satisfied, 2 (5%) were uncertain, and 1 (2.5%) was very dissatisfied. Corresponding figures for the OC-danazol group were 6 (15%), 17 (42.5%), 4 (10%), 12 (30%), and 1 (2.5%). Overall, 72.5% of women in the DMPA group compared with 57.5% of those in the OC group were pleased after 1 year of treatment. It was concluded that, in selected women with highly symptomatic endometriosis, DMPA offers good analgesic results with tolerable side effects."
    }
